{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pressure Unit Conversion.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pressure Unit Conversion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pressure unit conversion pressure unit conversion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pressure Unit Conversion.md","fileName":"Pressure Unit Conversion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pressure Unit Conversion","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Force Unit Conversion","Area Unit Scaling","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pressure Unit Conversion.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pressure Unit Conversion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pressure unit conversion pressure unit conversion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pressure Unit Conversion.md","fileName":"Pressure Unit Conversion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pressure Unit Conversion","heading":"Pressure Unit Conversion","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Pressure and stress are represented in the book by force per area, such as pound-force per square inch or newtons per square meter. Its psi-to-kPa example converts the force with $\\mathrm{N/lbf}$, converts the square-inch denominator by an inverse area factor, then changes pascals to kilopascals. Each step has a distinct dimensional role. Converting pound-force without the squared area would not yield pressure. See [[Force Unit Conversion]] and [[Area Unit Scaling]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Engineering and Scientific Unit Conversions","Force Unit Conversion","Area Unit Scaling","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"page